sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0248 Cluster router resolver amendment; one shared member policy for peer reconcile, converge and Cluster lifecycle.
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten rules 6, 22, 27, 28: persistence, live apply and check form one Ansible phase.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · managed DNS policy changes
  %% kind: fleet.peer_dns
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh
  Note over GW: Render changed policy<br/>Cluster and VPN scopes
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - an active, managed Linux Node with an active Cluster membership and a LAN address selects its Cluster's ready LAN Router, including the Router itself
  %%> - all other managed peers keep the VPN split policy; unsupported link ownership or a conflicting explicit DNS override refuses before mutation
  %%> - a roleless member needs managed SSH enrollment, not a workload role; unmanaged devices are reported without claiming convergence
  %%> Result:
  %%> - a complete per-Node projection, revision and digest, including the public resolver invariant
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-fleet-peer-dns
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - LAN server, owned suffixes, remaining VPN domains, probes and pinned SSH connection; no global resolver replacement
  %%> - the same projection is passed to this phase by the shared DNS publication during a Cluster transition
  Note over SW: Write the intent
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - ships the playbook and private run file; a matching policy still gets its end check
  SW->>N: Apply member DNS policy<br/>Ansible phase
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - resolves the LAN interface from the recorded LAN address and routing table; validates the active network manager and public DNS path before writing
  %%> - snapshots only owned policy, persists it through link recreation and reboot, applies live scope selection and flushes affected cached answers
  %%> - leaves addresses, routes, DHCP, resolv.conf and global resolved settings alone
  %%> Open:
  %%> - direct LAN per-link DNS is supported only with a separate public DNS scope; same-link public DNS needs the ADR's scope decision
  %%> - runtime VPN fallback is a separate open decision; two unequal servers are not an ordered failback mechanism
  alt policy and answers match
    %% path: succeeded
    N-->>SW: Policy checked<br/>revision · digest · scope
    %%> What it proves:
    %%> - Cluster A uses LAN and AAAA is NODATA; another private name uses VPN and a public name retains its ordinary resolver
    %%> - on withdrawal the Cluster suffix uses VPN before LAN policy is removed; no duplicate best-match domain remains
    %%> - an unchanged policy records a successful skip without a resolver reload
  else apply or check fails
    %% path: failed
    Note over N: Restore owned policy
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - restores captured persistent and live settings within the peer lock; leaves later revisions untouched
    %%> - failed recovery keeps its snapshot and reports dns.rollback_failed; the caller cannot retire the old Router
    N--xSW: Policy failed<br/>target · error_code
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - retain the failed target and repeat after repair; no workload or public DNS configuration is deleted
  end
  Note over SW: Remove the run folder
  %%> How:
  %%> - native completed dependencies wait for the phase, then remove disposable files on either outcome
  alt phase passed
    %% path: recorded
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal result
    %%> Result:
    %%> - records the applied policy; lifecycle callers can release their member cleanup barrier
  else phase failed
    %% path: refused
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step · error_code
    %%> Result:
    %%> - records drift and retains the unfinished transition; no separate member queue owns a Cluster handover
  end
